.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt PASSWD 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm passwd ,
.Nm shadow ,
.Nm group
.Nd account files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /etc/passwd
.Pp
.Pa /etc/shadow
.Pp
.Pa /etc/group
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The account files contain one record per line, with fields separated
by colons.
The project account tools limit names to 63 bytes and passwords to
100 bytes.
BusyBox's
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr su 1 ,
and
.Xr passwd 1
read the same files with shadow password and SHA crypt support.
.Ss Passwd records
.Pa /etc/passwd
has mode 0644.
Each record has seven fields:
.Dl name:x:uid:gid:gecos:home:shell
The fields give the account name, a marker for the password in
.Pa /etc/shadow ,
the numeric user and group IDs, descriptive text, the home directory,
and the login shell.
.Ss Shadow records
.Pa /etc/shadow
is accessible only to root.
Each record has nine fields:
.Dl name:hash:lastchg:min:max:warn:inactive:expire:flag
The first two fields are the account name and password hash.
SHA-512 crypt hashes have the form
.Ql $6$salt$... .
A field beginning with
.Ql \&!
locks password authentication.
The project credential library also rejects an empty hash field.
BusyBox's login and su accept an empty field without a password.
.Pp
The remaining fields record the last password change, minimum and maximum
password ages, warning period, inactivity period, account expiration,
and a reserved flag field.
The project
.Xr adduser 8
writes them empty:
.Dl name:!:::::::
The project
.Xr passwd 1
requires these fields to be empty or decimal and preserves them when
replacing the hash.
The project credential library reads the hash for authentication;
it does not enforce the aging fields.
.Ss Group records
.Pa /etc/group
has records of the form:
.Dl name:x:gid:members
The fields give the group name, a password placeholder, the numeric
group ID, and the member list.
.Ss Credential transactions
Project credential writers create
.Pa /etc/.pwlock
with mode 0600 and record their process ID in it.
A valid lock contains one positive decimal PID of at most nine digits
followed only by spaces, tabs, carriage returns, or newlines.
Validation reads the complete file and rejects embedded NUL bytes.
They do not remove a lock left by another writer, even if that process
has died.
After a killed writer, root removes the abandoned lock with:
.Dl rm /etc/.pwlock
.Pp
A replacement for
.Ar FILE
is written to
.Pa FILE.tmp.PID
and renamed over the original.
Readers take no lock and see either complete version of the file.
Failure before rename leaves the target unchanged and attempts to remove
the temporary file.
A failed PID write likewise attempts to remove the new lock.
Removal errors report the file requiring manual cleanup.
Transaction release retries temporary-file removal and returns failure
if removal still fails.
.Pp
Immediate exit, SIGKILL, CPU faults, and musl-reserved signals 32 through 34
can bypass cleanup.
Release checks the recorded PID before unlinking the lock; manual removal
and replacement between the check and unlink can still remove a successor's lock.
.Pp
The project
.Xr adduser 8
publishes a locked shadow record first, then the group record, then
the home directory with mode 0700, and finally the passwd record.
An interruption therefore leaves no passwd account without a locked
shadow record.
.Ss Demonstration accounts
The os-simple and os-real images contain the demonstration accounts
.Ql root
and
.Ql test .
The installer replaces the demonstration account records in the
installed root.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/.pwlock
.It Pa /etc/passwd
Account names, identities, home directories, and shells.
.It Pa /etc/shadow
Password hashes and aging fields.
.It Pa /etc/group
Group identities and members.
.It Pa /etc/.pwlock
Project credential writers' lock.
.It Pa FILE.tmp.PID
Replacement for an account file, before rename.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr su 1 ,
.Xr sshd.conf 5 ,
.Xr adduser 8
